<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <UseArtifactsOutput>true</UseArtifactsOutput>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
    <!--
      `latest` here means "whatever the installed SDK considers current", and combined with
      TreatWarningsAsErrors above that makes the set of rules able to fail this build a property of
      the toolchain. It stays `latest` on purpose: global.json pins the SDK to one feature band
      (rollForward=latestPatch) and CI installs from that file, so `latest` now resolves to a version
      this repo chose. Pinning a second number here (10.0-recommended) would be a duplicate of that
      decision, free to drift from it, and the drift would be silent.

      The consequence to keep in mind when reviewing the Dependabot SDK bump: THAT PR is where the
      recommended rule set can widen, so it is the one that can go red on files it does not touch.
      That is the intended place for it to happen. See .github/dependabot.yml.
    -->
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    dotnet build -p:FunctionalBuild=true — the same binaries without the build-time gates: no
    analyzers, no code-style enforcement, no XML documentation files. Nothing reads the XML docs and
    the gates emit diagnostics, not code, so the test binaries are identical; the build is ~25s faster
    on a 2-vCPU runner.

    IT COVERS THIS REPOSITORY'S PROJECTS, NOT THE VENDORED .deps/wham. Those projects evaluate the
    submodule's own Directory.Build.props, which does not import this one, so its analyzers
    (AnalysisMode=All) and XML docs still run under the switch: ~2s of wham's build on the dev box at
    -m:2. The three raw flags this replaces were global properties and did reach wham, which is why
    the build they measured (81s against 108-113s for full builds, run 36772377382) is a few seconds
    better than this switch should be expected to match. Reaching wham would take a mechanism that
    rides on the submodule's layout (a root Directory.Build.targets applies only while wham has none
    of its own) for those few seconds, so it stays out.

    CI uses it for every build except the `checks` job, which IS the analyzer gate; a lint
    (CiWorkflowDriftTests.Checks_BuildsWithAnalyzers) fails if `checks` ever sets it, and another
    (OtherCiBuilds_UseFunctionalBuild) if any other ci.yml build does not. It replaces three raw
    `-p:` flags that one job spelled out and the others did not, and the properties below are the
    one record of what it turns off: the lint that bans the raw flags in CI reads them from here, and
    fails if any of these three is missing from it. Locally it is a faster inner loop; run a plain
    `dotnet build` before pushing, because the analyzers are part of what `checks` will hold you to.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(FunctionalBuild)' == 'true'">
    <RunAnalyzers>false</RunAnalyzers>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>false</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Test profiles: dotnet test -p:TestProfile=nr-live -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TestProfile)' != ''">
    <RunSettingsFilePath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)tests\test-profiles\$(TestProfile).runsettings</RunSettingsFilePath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="_ValidateTestProfile" BeforeTargets="VSTest" Condition="'$(TestProfile)' != '' AND '$(IsTestProject)' == 'true'">
    <Error Text="Unknown test profile '$(TestProfile)'. Available profiles: $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)tests\test-profiles\*.runsettings"
           Condition="!Exists('$(RunSettingsFilePath)')" />
  </Target>
</Project>
